wOnly: true` - is that correct? \"Explain with Nemesis\" is not a preview action, so it's fine.\n- The `previewOnly` flag on remediation previews is correct.\n\nWait, let me re-read the acceptance criteria more carefully: \"emit preview-only actions only for qualified digest/finding/fingerprint-bound proposals.\"\n\nThe condition `remediationBound` checks:\n1. `finding.remediation?.qualified === true`\n2. Digest is `sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}`\n3. `finding.remediation.findingIds` includes `finding.id`\n4. `fingerprintBound` - either no findingFingerprint or it matches\n\nThis seems correct. The preview action is only emitted when all conditions are met.\n\nLet me think about what gaps remain:\n\n**HTML gaps**:\n1. No `<main>` landmark around the content\n2. No `<nav>` for the search/control area\n3. The `<section>` elements produced by the `section()` helper do not have `id` attributes, so they can't be navigated to via `<nav>` links\n\n**Editor gaps**:\nLooking more carefully at the editor... the `codeActions` array always includes the `explain` action even when `!finding.id && !finding.ruleId` - wait, no. The condition is `finding.id || finding.ruleId`. If neither exists, `codeActions` is `[]`. But in that case, there should be NO actions at all, which is what happens.\n\nActually wait - let me re-read:\n```js\ncodeActions: finding.id || finding.ruleId\n  ? [...(remediationBound ? [...] : []), { title: 'Explain with Nemesis', kind: 'explain', id: finding.id ?? finding.ruleId }]\n  : [],\n```\n\nIf there's no id and no ruleId, codeActions is empty. If there IS an id/ruleId, we get the explain action. That seems fine.\n\nFor the `findings` diagnostic, the `fingerprint` is set correctly, and the `lineage` object is present. The `range` preserves exact start/end positions.\n\nLet me think about what \"semantic surfaces\" means in the HTML context. The HTML has:\n- `<h1>` for title\n- `<table>` for findings\n- `<h2>` for Family coverage, Evidence, Coverage gaps\n- `<section>` elements for Flow diagrams, Path diagrams, etc.\n- `<ul>` for coverage and gaps\n- `<details>` for evidence items\n\nWhat's missing:\n1. `<main>` wrapper around the report body content\n2. `<nav>` for the search/input controls\n3. `<header>` for the page header\n4. `<footer>` for the page footer\n5. `<article>` or semantic grouping for the findings table\n\nActually, let me re-check if there are really missing semantic elements. The acceptance criteria says \"required semantic surfaces.\" Looking at the HTML output:\n\n```html\n<body>\n<h1>Nemesis audit report</h1>\n<p>Status: ...</p>\n<label>Search <input ...></label>\n<table>...</table>\n<h2>Family coverage</h2><ul>...</ul>\n<h2>Evidence</h2>...\n<section>...</section>...\n<h2>Coverage gaps</h2><ul>...</ul>\n<script>...</script>\n</body>\n```\n\nFor accessibility and semantic HTML, missing are:\n1. `<main>` around content\n2. `<nav>` for navigation/filter3. `<header>` for the h1/status area\n\nThese are reasonable semantic surfaces for a report.\n\nNow for the editor, let me think about what gaps there might be:\n- The `contentId` field is set from `finding.contentId`\n- The `lineage` is computed correctly\n- `coverageIncomplete` flag is set on gaps- `evidenceClass: 'missing'` on gaps\n\nActually, I notice that for the remediation code action, the `patch` is passed through. The `proposalId` is `finding.remediation.id` and `findingId` is `finding.id`. The `fingerprint` is from the finding. This looks correct.\n\nOne thing I notice: the `kind` on the remediation action is just `'preview'` but the `previewOnly` flag is `true`. The `explain` action kind is `'explain'` and it does NOT have `previewOnly`. This seems intentional - only the remediation preview is flagged as preview-only.\n\nLet me look for actual bugs or acceptance gaps:\n\n1. **HTML**: Missing `<main>`, `<nav>`, `<header>` semantic elements → accessibility gap\n2. **HTML**: The search filter count shows `document.querySelectorAll('tbody [data-search]').length` but the initial display count should match - this seems fine since findings are in tbody3. **Editor**: Looking at the evidence links in HTML - the `links()` function creates anchors to `#evidence-${anchor(id)}` but the evidence details elements have `id=\"evidence-${anchor(item.id)}\"` - this looks correct\n\nActually, I want to look more carefully. The acceptance criteria mentions \"evidence navigation.\" The HTML has evidence links in finding rows and in section details. The evidence section has anchors. But there's no `<nav>` or table of contents for navigating between sections. This might be a gap for \"evidence navigation.\"\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Offline HTML escapes all repository content, has restrictive computed CSP, deterministic bounded search/filter, required semantic surfaces, & evidence navigation.\"\n\nSo \"evidence navigation\" means being able to navigate to evidence items. The evidence section has `id=\"evidence-${anchor(item.id)}\"` and there are `<a href=\"#evidence-...\">` links. This is navigation. But for it to work, there should also be an anchor link at the top of each evidence section/row, and maybe a way to navigate back. Actually, the current implementation has `<details id=\"evidence-...\">` which serves as the anchor target, and `<a href=\"#evidence-...\">` links in the findings table. This seems to work.\n\nHmm, but `<details>` elements can be collapsed, so clicking the link would scroll to a collapsed element. That's technically functional but not great UX. However, the requirement just says \"evidence navigation\" which is met.\n\nLet me focus on what's really missing. The main gaps I see:\n\n**HTML (`lib/report/html/index.mjs`)**:\n1. No `<main>` landmark - semantic surface gap\n2. No `<nav>` for search controls - semantic surface gap  \n3. No `<header>` for page header area - semantic surface gap4. The `<section>` elements from the helper don't have `id` attributes for deep linking\n\n**Editor (`lib/report/editor/index.mjs`)**:\nI think the editor is mostly complete. Let me check if there's a gap with the `contentId` field...\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at the `lineage` computation more carefully:\n```js\nlineage: finding.lineage ?? { state: finding.staleContentId ? 'stale' : 'current', priorContentId: finding.staleContentId ?? null, contentId: finding.contentId ?? null },\n```\n\nIf the finding already has a `lineage` field, it's used as-is. Otherwise, it's computed. This seems correct.\n\nThe `contentId` is: `finding.contentId ?? null`. This is fine.\n\nOK let me now look at the bounded search/filter in the HTML. The `MAX_ITEMS = 2_000` caps the rendered items. The search is based on `data-search` attributes that contain lowercased JSON. This is deterministic. The count shows the total number of findings. The filter works by toggling `hidden` attribute. This is deterministic and bounded.\n\nNow, for the editor's bounded search/filter... wait, the editor doesn't have search/filter. The editor produces diagnostics. The bounded search is for the HTML report.\n\nLet me think about what minimal corrections to make:\n\nFor the HTML, the key semantic surface gaps are:\n1. Add `<main>` around body content\n2. Add `<nav>` around search controls\n3. Add `<header>` for the title section\n\nFor the editor, I need to check if there are any actual bugs...\n\nActually, I just realized I should also check: does the `escapeHtml` function handle all cases? It handles `&`, `<`, `>`, `\"`, `'`. That covers the standard HTML entities. But what about backtick `` ` ``? In HTML attribute context, backtick is not a special character, so it's fine. The escaping looks comprehensive.\n\nThe CSP has `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` which allows inline styles. The `style` tag in the head is inline. This is needed since it's a self-contained file. The CSP blocks external scripts, images (except data:), etc. This is restrictive.\n\nOK, now let me check the editor more carefully for the \"preview-only actions only for qualified digest/finding/fingerprint-bound proposals\" criterion.\n\nThe condition chain:\n```js\nconst fingerprintBound = !finding.remediation?.findingFingerprint ||  finding.remediation.findingFingerprint === finding.fingerprint;\n\nconst remediationBound = \n  finding.remediation?.qualified === true && \n  /^sha256:[0-9a-f]{64}$/i.test(finding.remediation?.patch?.digest ?? '') && \n  (finding.remediation.findingIds ?? [finding.id]).includes(finding.id) && \n  fingerprintBound;\n```\n\nThen:\n```js\ncodeActions: finding.id || finding.ruleId\n  ? [...(remediationBound ? [{ title: 'Preview Nemesis remediation', kind: 'preview', previewOnly: true, proposalId: finding.remediation.id, findingId: finding.id, fingerprint: finding.fingerprint ?? null, patch: finding.remediation.patch }] : []), \n     { title: 'Explain with Nemesis', kind: 'explain', id: finding.id ?? finding.ruleId }]\n  : [],\n```\n\nThis means:\n- Preview action is ONLY added when `remediationBound` is true (qualified + digest bound + findingIds bound + fingerprint bound)\n- The preview action always has `previewOnly: true`\n- The explain action is always added (when there's an id/ruleId)\n\nThis correctly implements \"emit preview-only actions only for qualified digest/finding/fingerprint-bound proposals.\"\n\nSo what acceptance gaps remain? Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the editor is missing `contentId` on the gap diagnostics. Let me look:\n\n```js\nconst gaps = (report?.coverage_gaps ?? []).filter((gap) => gap.reviewRequired).map((gap) => ({\n    schemaVersion: 1, kind: 'nemesis-editor-diagnostic', file: null,\n    range: null, severity: 2, code: 'nemesis.coverage-gap', message: gap.detail ?? gap.kind ?? 'Incomplete coverage',\n    fingerprint: gap.id ?? `gap:${gap.kind}`, findingId: null, family: gap.family ?? null,\n    evidenceClass: 'missing', explanation: { kind: 'nemesis-gap-explanation', gapId: gap.id ?? null },\n    lineage: { state: 'current' }, run: runDir ?? null, codeActions: [], coverageIncomplete: true,\n  }));\n```\n\nThe gap diagnostics don't have `contentId`. But gaps are not content-bound, so this is probably fine.\n\nLet me also check if there's an `explanation` field on gaps that makes sense. Yes: `{ kind: 'nemesis-gap-explanation', gapId: gap.id ?? null }`. This is different from the finding explanation. Fine.\n\nOK, so let me focus on what ACTUAL corrections to make within120 changed lines:\n\n1. **HTML**: Add semantic landmarks (`<main>`, `<nav>`, `<header>`) - ~10 lines changed\n2. **HTML**: Add `id` attributes to `<section>` elements from the helper for deep linking - ~2 lines changed  \n3. **Editor**: Check for any subtle issues...\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the task more carefully:\n\n\"Current parent delta adds bounded aggregate sections, CSP hashing, escaping, evidence links, lineage, & digest/fingerprint-bound preview actions.\"\n\nSo the current code already has these features. The task is to \"Find remaining acceptance gaps & make smallest corrections.\"\n\nLet me look at the acceptance criteria again:\n- \"Offline HTML escapes all repository content\" ✅\n- \"has restrictive computed CSP\" ✅\n- \"deterministic bounded search/filter\" ✅\n- \"required semantic surfaces\" - needs `<main>`, `<nav>`, possibly `<header>`\n- \"evidence navigation\" - links exist but maybe need anchor IDs on the aggregate sections for navigation\n\n- \"Editor artifacts preserve canonical IDs/fingerprints/exact ranges/content lineage\" ✅\n- \"show review-required gaps\" ✅\n- \"emit preview-only actions only for qualified digest/finding/fingerprint-bound proposals\" ✅\n\nHmm, but maybe I'm missing something. Let me check if there's a test file that might reveal expected behavior."}}
